# FDD 29 review — "29 - Educator Credentialing - Certificates" (Features 29.1-29.4).
# Ingested per abox/mied-v3/fdd-review/CONVENTIONS.md against the mied-v3 Credentialing
# graph (credentialing-domain.ttl / credentialing-permissions.ttl / credentialing-api.ttl /
# credentialing-sequences.ttl / credentialing-technical-design.ttl, all loaded together).
#
# Source: "29 - Educator Credentialing - Certificate.docx". Read the .md sibling first,
# then unzipped the .docx for comments.xml/commentsExtended.xml (thread structure via
# w15:paraIdParent), document.xml (embedded drawings + a handful of tracked changes), and
# word/media (10 captioned "Wireframe N" images, image1.png-image10.png) plus a top-level
# /media folder (18 further, UNCAPTIONED higher-fidelity screenshots embedded directly in
# the "Addendum" Feature 29.1-29.4 write-up, referenced via odd absolute-path relationship
# targets like "/media/image.jpg" rather than the normal "media/imageN.png" pattern used
# by the 10 captioned ones — a packaging quirk, not a modeling concern).
#
# Structure of the document itself, and what's used as the Requirement unit:
# - A "Business Specifications" prose section + a "HIGH LEVEL SCENARIOS" paraphrase table
#   (29 rows) both restate the same ~10 landing-page actions at coarser grain — the HLS
#   table is skipped per CONVENTIONS.md (duplicative of the prose, itself superseded by
#   the Addendum below for everything the Addendum actually numbers).
# - An "Addendum" with real Feature/story numbering (29.1.1-29.1.10, 29.2.1-29.2.10,
#   29.3.1-29.3.11, 29.4.1-29.4.5) is the primary Requirement unit used below.
# - Three real, wireframed capabilities described in the Business Specifications prose
#   (Add College Credits, Add DPPD / view PD-hours-earned, Complete SCECH Evaluations)
#   were NEVER promoted into the Addendum's numbered Feature breakdown at all — no
#   29.x number exists for them anywhere in the document, despite each having its own
#   field list and captioned wireframe (Wireframe 5, 6, and the SCECH table respectively).
#   Modeled below with synthetic cred:Req_29_BizSpec_* IDs and flagged as a discrepancy
#   (Question_AddendumOmitsThreeBizSpecFeatures) rather than silently absorbed into the
#   Addendum's own numbering scheme.
# - Feature 29.4's own numbering is internally inconsistent: a heading "29.4.3 View
#   Enrollment Information" is followed by body text numbered "29.4.3" that is actually
#   the Confirm-Enrollment story, then a second story numbered "29.4.4" that is actually
#   the enrollment-information-table story, then a second heading/story ALSO numbered
#   "29.4.4" for View Pending Certificate Applications. The review comment thread (see
#   Question_ConfirmEnrollmentLateAddition below) shows why: Confirm Enrollment was
#   added late, in response to a reviewer comment, without renumbering what follows.
#   Modeled below as Req_29_4_3 (Confirm Enrollment), Req_29_4_3b (View Enrollment
#   Information table), Req_29_4_4 (View Pending Certificate Applications) — the FDD's
#   own literal numbers are preserved in sd:requirementId, collision and all.
#
# Comments (27 total, read via comments.xml + commentsExtended.xml's w15:paraIdParent
# thread links, not just flattened in isolation): all resolved/reply-only threads are
# folded into the relevant Requirement's own sd:notes rather than transcribed verbatim.
# Two threads surfaced substantial, still-live findings and are modeled as standalone
# Annotations: the "cover letter — do we still need this story?" thread (see
# Req_29_4_2 and Question_CoverLetterStoryContradiction — the single most important
# finding of this pass) and the "confirm enrollment late addition" thread. A handful of
# smaller single-comment findings (PKS test type introduced mid-review, still-unresolved
# "Needs Responses"/in-state-out-of-state flags, still-missing full-enrollment-list
# wireframe) are each their own Annotation since they're genuinely distinct points, not
# near-duplicates of each other the way FDD 11/16's "define role" thread was.
#
# Tracked changes: only 5 w:ins/4 w:del spans in the whole document (far fewer than FDD
# 11/16's ~120) — not reconstructed as a diff; nothing load-bearing found in a manual scan.
#
# Wireframes: the 10 captioned "Wireframe N" images (word/media/image1.png-image10.png,
# in strict document order matching their captions 1-10 — confirmed by viewing all 10,
# not assumed) are extracted to wireframes/wireframe-01-*.png through wireframe-10-*.png.
# Of the 18 further uncaptioned addendum screenshots, 5 were viewed to confirm general
# shape/fidelity (a higher-fidelity, closer-to-final-product UI than the boxy "Wireframe
# N" images — tabs/breadcrumbs/real component styling) and are extracted as
# wireframes/addendum-{a-e}-*.{jpg,png}; one further addendum image (image5.jpg) was
# confirmed byte-identical to image.jpg (the Credentials-landing screenshot reused
# verbatim) and not re-extracted. The remaining ~12 addendum images were not individually
# viewed — per CONVENTIONS.md's depth-calibration guidance, enough were sampled across
# the document's span (early/mid/late) to confirm they're all real product screenshots of
# the same application/payment/credential-list flows already covered by the ones that
# were viewed, not a distinct undocumented feature.
#
# Verification task (per this review's own brief): three existing credentialing-domain.ttl
# BusinessRule citations of FDD 29 were checked against the actual FDD text, not just
# trusted:
# - Rule_CertificateDocumentGeneration's citation of "Feature 29.1.6, 29.1.9" for the
#   blank-issuance/expiration-date rendering rule on Revoked/Nullified/Suspended
#   certificates: CONFIRMED ACCURATE. Both stories state it near-verbatim ("Issue date
#   and expiration date (or blank for revoked/nullified/suspended per main spec)" at
#   29.1.6; "The system visually distinguishes revoked, suspended, or nullified
#   certificates (as their dates may be blank)" at 29.1.9).
# - Rule_AutoApprovalCriteria's citation of "Feature 29.3" for endorsement-addition
#   applications being exempt from the PPR-clearance auto-approval criterion: CONFIRMED,
#   WITH A NUANCE WORTH FLAGGING. 29.3.1-29.3.10 do consistently omit any PPR/Needs-
#   Responses routing language (unlike every certificate-type flow in the main Business
#   Specifications section, which explicitly lists "Route or assign the application to
#   worklist(s) based on the PPR flag" for every certificate type). But 29.3.11 (Route
#   Endorsement Application) adds: "Routing considers applicable PPR workflows where
#   required (if in future scope)" — a hedge that says PPR routing for endorsements may
#   be added later, which the domain rule's "confirmed" framing doesn't currently account
#   for. See Question_EndorsementPprHedgeIn29311 below.
# - credentialing-technical-design.ttl's "Related, not yet modeled: Cover Letter
#   Generation" note (citing FDD 29 Feature 29.1/29.4.2): re-examined against the actual
#   document plus its own review-comment thread. NOT simply "underspecified" as the
#   existing note frames it — see Question_CoverLetterStoryContradiction. The document
#   still carries a full 29.4.2 write-up AND a landing-page wireframe (Wireframe 1) with
#   a live "Print Cover Letter" button, but a 3-comment reviewer thread on that exact
#   story appears to agree the standalone cover-letter-PDF capability isn't needed at all
#   ("we are not going to generate cover letter and it is going to be part of email" /
#   "Agree" / "Got it") — and was never reflected back into the visible text. This is a
#   genuine, unresolved contradiction within the FDD's own paper trail, not a "not enough
#   detail yet" situation — drafting a TechnicalProcess from this evidence now would risk
#   building the wrong thing (or the right thing at the wrong scope — the wireframe's own
#   caption narrows cover-letter printing to "out-of-state applications" specifically,
#   which 29.4.2's acceptance criteria never mentions).

@prefix sd:    <urn:solutiondesign:ontology:> .
@prefix oa:    <http://www.w3.org/ns/oa#> .
@prefix rdfs:  <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix cred:  <urn:solutiondesign:instance:credentialing:> .
@prefix epp:   <urn:solutiondesign:instance:epp:> .
@prefix plrn:  <urn:solutiondesign:instance:proflearning:> .
@prefix docs:  <urn:solutiondesign:instance:documents:> .
@prefix pay:   <urn:solutiondesign:instance:payments:> .

#################################################################
# Source documents
#################################################################

